    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d673">

                <pb facs="0612" n="606" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg606"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.20/478½</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Acting Chief of the Division of Near
                        Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Alling</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">and the Chief of the Division of Controls</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Green</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-07-28">July 28, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Liberian Government fears the possibility of an attack upon the capital by
                    Germans resident in the country. We have reason to believe that these fears may
                    not be entirely unjustified.</p>
                <p>The Liberian Frontier Force, as at present armed, would probably find it
                    difficult to cope with a well-organized coup on the part of German residents. It
                    would, however, be in a position to cope with such a coup and probably to
                    prevent it if the Frontier Force were increased from its present strength of 650
                    to 1,000 and if it could obtain 15 or 20 machine guns. An American negro, a
                    former American Army officer, has been retained by the Liberian Government as
                        <pb facs="0613" n="607" xml:id="pg607"/> a military adviser. He is competent
                    to train the Liberians in the use of machine guns.</p>
                <p>The Colt’s Patent Fire Arms Manufacturing Company has informed the Department it
                    would be prepared to sell and deliver immediately to the Liberian Government
                    fully equipped machine guns at the nominal price of $200 per gun.</p>
                <p>This memorandum has been seen and approved by the Acting Secretary of the Navy
                    and the Chief of Staff of the Army.</p>
                <closer><signed><list>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Paul H. Alling</hi></item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Green</hi></item>
                        </list></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
